XPS Thickness Suite - attribution and licensing notes
=====================================================

Thickogram (thickogram.py, thickogram_tab.py, legacy/thickogram_v1_3.py)
  - Method: Peter J. Cumpson, "The Thickogram: a method for easy film thickness measurement in XPS",
    Surf. Interface Anal. 29, 403-406 (2000).
  - The nomogram code derives from the GlowScript (WebVPython) program written by Prof. Jong Wan Lee,
    Hallym University, School of Nano Convergence Technology, Korea
    (https://www.glowscript.org/#/user/jwsslee/folder/Private/program/thickogram), converted to Python/Tkinter
    in the aktiv8/Thickogram repository (https://github.com/aktiv8/Thickogram).
  - LICENCE NOTE: the licence of the original GlowScript program is not known to the maintainer of this
    repository, and the aktiv8/Thickogram repository carries no licence file. The derived code is included here
    with attribution. If you are the author and have concerns, please contact the repository owner.

Topofactors (topofactor.py, the sample-shape controls of the Thickogram tab)
  - Method and data: A. G. Shard, J. Wang and S. J. Spencer, "XPS topofactors: determining overlayer thickness on
    particles and fibres", Surf. Interface Anal. 41, 541-548 (2009), DOI 10.1002/sia.3044. The Topofactor definition,
    the thin-film limits (2/pi, 0.50, 0.57), the constant averages (0.79, 0.67, 0.71), Eq. (6) and the shape
    parameters of its Table 1 are taken from that paper; the implementation is original.

ARXPS tab (arxps.py, arxps_tab.py, vamas.py, casa.py, synthetic.py)
  - Methods: angle-resolved XPS as in P. J. Cumpson, J. Electron Spectrosc. Relat. Phenom. 73, 25 (1995) and ISO 14701;
    Shirley background: D. A. Shirley, Phys. Rev. B 5, 4709 (1972); VAMAS format: W. A. Dench, L. B. Hazell and
    M. P. Seah, Surf. Interface Anal. 13, 63 (1988). The implementation is original.
  - casa.py (CasaXPS peak fits stored in VAMAS comments) is a trimmed port of the comment parser of eXPoSe SpectraDeck
    (casafit.py and quant.py), https://github.com/aktiv8/eXPoSe-Spectra-Deck, written by the repository owner. That
    project carries no licence file; only the parser is used here, none of its line-shape tables. CasaXPS is a product
    of Casa Software Ltd.

Depth profile tab (depthprofile.py, depth_tab.py)
  - The methods follow the ideas of the NPL ARCtick package (P. J. Cumpson, J. Electron Spectrosc. Relat. Phenom. 73, 25
    (1995)); the Surrey Trend Plot was suggested by J. Castle and J. Watts (University of Surrey). The code is an
    independent implementation from the published physics: no code, text or images of ARCtick are used, because ARCtick
    is distributed under a notice that it is not to be copied without the permission of NPL.
  - Profile (Murdoch) (depthmurdoch.py): the method of B. J. Murdoch and D. G. McCulloch, Surf. Interface Anal. 55, 658
    (2023), DOI 10.1002/sia.7240, implemented independently from the equations of the paper. The authors' spreadsheet
    was used only to check the numbers and is not included.

Strohmeier calculator and presets
  - Equation: B. R. Strohmeier, Surf. Interface Anal. 15, 51-56 (1990); HarwellXPS Technical Note #1.
  - Al / Al2O3 presets: Strohmeier (1990). Cu oxide/hydroxide presets: M. C. Biesinger, Surf. Interface Anal. 49,
    1325 (2017). W / WO3 presets: T. A. Carlson and G. E. McGuire, J. Electron Spectrosc. Relat. Phenom. 1, 161
    (1972/73). Al oxide/hydroxide cross-check: M. C. Biesinger's thickness spreadsheet (Surface Science Western, 2008).
  - Densities and band gaps for Si, SiO2, Si3N4, Ti, TiO2 and several others come from the Thermo Avantage
    Densities.txt file supplied by the repository owner. Other densities are standard handbook values, and the
    IMFPs marked "computed" use the TPP-2M formula (Tanuma, Powell and Penn, Surf. Interface Anal. 21, 165 (1994)).
